    <h2>A Layered Cup, Not a Blizzard</h2>
<p>The key difference between the Cupfection and a <a href="/items/oreo-blizzard">Blizzard</a> is construction. A Blizzard is blended so every spoonful is uniform. The Cupfection is layered, so you move through distinct strata — soft serve, then a band of brownie, then Oreo and fudge. If you like variety within a single cup and enjoy hitting a warm brownie pocket, the Cupfection delivers something the Blizzard cannot.</p>
<h2>Is It Worth $6.29?</h2>
<p>At $6.29 and 820 calories, the Cupfection is one of the pricier and richest single treats at DQ. Compared with the <a href="/items/peanut-buster-parfait">Peanut Buster Parfait</a> ($4.99, 710 cal), you are paying about $1.30 more for the brownie-and-Oreo combination instead of peanuts. It earns the premium if brownie is your priority; if you are cost-focused, the Parfait is the better value in the same layered-cup family.</p>
<h2>Sharing and Portion Notes</h2>
<p>820 calories is a lot for one person as an after-meal dessert. It splits well between two, and it travels better than a cone or a <a href="/items/banana-split">Banana Split</a> because everything is contained in a cup. If you want the same flavor profile in blended form, order an Oreo Blizzard and add a brownie mix-in instead.</p>
